<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parthian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Parthians, Parthian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parthian Empire, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsacid-dynasty">Arsacid dynasty</a>, was an Iranian state that emerged around 247 BCE and came to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. It replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> in the east and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s main rival, while controlling trade along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. It was overthrown in 224 CE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parthian-empire.jpg" alt="Parthian Empire" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original file by Ro4444, edited by me · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Parthian_Empire_under_Mithridates_II.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…credits him with chariots and 12,000 cavalry, most likely heavy cavalry or cataphracts of the kind the Seleucids and <b>Parthians</b> used, and his force consisted mainly of 120,000 infantry and 12,000 mounted archers, a Parthian feature too; like the Seleucids he relied on foot soldiers for the bulk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the first century CE King Pharasmanes I pursued an aggressive policy: in 35 CE, with Roman encouragement, he seized Armenia for his brother Mithridates and brought Sarmatian allies through the passes to defeat the <b>Parthians</b>, and his son Rhadamistus later ruled Armenia for a few turbulent years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kujula Kadphises built the empire by conquest, defeating the Indo-<b>Parthians</b> and taking Kabul, Gandhara and probably Taxila, while his successors pushed into the Punjab, Mathura and further down the Ganges.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the second century BCE, displaced Saka warriors overran parts of Bactria and fought the <b>Parthians</b>, killing King Phraates II in about 127 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Manichaeism was founded by Mani, who was born in 216 in Mesopotamia, then part of the <b>Parthian Empire</b> and soon of the Sasanian Empire, near its capital Ctesiphon.</blockquote><a href="/religion/manichaeism">Manichaeism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Parthians</b> a revival in Assyria, from the 2nd century BCE to the 4th century CE, dedicated temples once more to Ashur, Sin, Shamash, Hadad, Bel and Ishtar.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion">Mesopotamian religion · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
